WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.384
This briefing comes from Book 01 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.384 --> 00:00:08.862
The Transformation Arc: the move from
a domain-joined client-server estate

3
00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:13.413
to cloud identity, cloud device management
and governed hybrid operations.

4
00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.548
Book 01 is the workbook's overview,

5
00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:19.362
and each part of it points to
the book that carries the detail.

6
00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:22.999
I'll take four questions:
why modernization is so hard,

7
00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:26.388
what has to change, who leads
it, and how to get there.

8
00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:31.790
The short version: modernization is a
governance rebuild, not a platform swap.

9
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.515
Why is modernization so hard?

10
00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:36.615
The estate got here in four eras.

11
00:00:36.615 --> 00:00:38.369
Each solved a real problem,

12
00:00:38.369 --> 00:00:42.753
and none replaced what the first
era had: one authoritative answer.

13
00:00:42.753 --> 00:00:47.190
On the mainframe, one machine held
the data and enforced access to it.

14
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.675
By the late 1980s, regional
databases and directories

15
00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:53.540
had spread that truth
across a dozen systems.

16
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.415
From 2010 the direction reversed.

17
00:00:56.415 --> 00:01:03.703
OMB reported that federal data centers had
grown from 432 in 1998 to more than 1,100

18
00:01:03.703 --> 00:01:10.670
in 2009, and the December 2010 reform plan
targeted closing at least 800 by 2015,

19
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.528
wiring offices back to distant facilities.

20
00:01:14.528 --> 00:01:17.666
Then email and collaboration
moved to the cloud,

21
00:01:17.666 --> 00:01:20.491
while identity stayed in regional domains.

22
00:01:20.491 --> 00:01:23.140
And each fix assumes the last one is done.

23
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.544
Zero Trust assumes governed identity,
governed identity assumes a clean directory,

24
00:01:28.544 --> 00:01:31.830
and a clean directory
assumes someone owns it.

25
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.843
Second, trust itself changed.

26
00:01:34.843 --> 00:01:38.765
In Active Directory a user signs
in once and gets a Kerberos

27
00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:43.729
ticket-granting ticket that lasts 10
hours by default and renews for 7 days.

28
00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:47.320
An open connection isn't
interrupted when its ticket expires,

29
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.755
and an account disabled during the day
can keep using tickets issued before.

30
00:01:51.755 --> 00:01:55.530
Zero Trust decides each
request against current policy.

31
00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.527
An Entra access token
lasts one hour by default,

32
00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:03.905
or up to 28 hours with continuous access
evaluation, because the service subscribes

33
00:02:03.905 --> 00:02:09.130
to events such as a disabled account and
stops honoring the token in near real time.

34
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.130
It's a badge checked at every door.

35
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.980
Distance doesn't break tickets.

36
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.217
It hurts the trips to a domain controller:
sign-in, password changes and policy.

37
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:21.944
On the book's worked arithmetic,
Portland to Washington has a

38
00:02:21.944 --> 00:02:26.400
floor of about 45 milliseconds
round trip, whatever the bandwidth.

39
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.475
Chapter 02 names the problem.

40
00:02:29.475 --> 00:02:32.921
Active Directory was the
implicit governance model for

41
00:02:32.921 --> 00:02:36.000
every Windows-era service,
and nobody wrote it down.

42
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.555
Microsoft's design guidance says
organizational units exist to apply

43
00:02:40.555 --> 00:02:45.188
Group Policy and delegate authority,
and that both inherit down the tree.

44
00:02:45.188 --> 00:02:48.862
So placing one user in an
OU did five things at once.

45
00:02:48.862 --> 00:02:52.064
It handed the account to that
branch's administrators,

46
00:02:52.064 --> 00:02:56.761
applied every policy linked along the
path, fed the tools that built groups from

47
00:02:56.761 --> 00:03:01.600
position, bound it to the branch's delegation
authority, and drew an audit boundary.

48
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.875
Entra ID has no OU tree.

49
00:03:03.875 --> 00:03:06.020
Dynamic groups follow attributes,

50
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.162
and administrative units scope
delegated roles but can't be nested.

51
00:03:10.162 --> 00:03:14.925
Neither rebuilds the tree on its own;
the rebuild has to be written down.

52
00:03:14.925 --> 00:03:18.827
And Microsoft still supports
and enhances Active Directory,

53
00:03:18.827 --> 00:03:22.860
so retiring it is an agency
decision, not a vendor deadline.

54
00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.688
Most "AD to Entra ID" projects
are scoped around users,

55
00:03:27.688 --> 00:03:30.722
groups and mailboxes, the visible part.

56
00:03:30.722 --> 00:03:37.238
The program counts eleven categories of
object that Active Directory governs: users,

57
00:03:37.238 --> 00:03:42.653
computers, service accounts, security
groups, Group Policy, DNS and DHCP,

58
00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:48.160
certificate services, RADIUS, LDAP,
service principal names and trusts.

59
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.030
Migrate the users into a flat directory,

60
00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.248
declare victory, and the
rest are left behind.

61
00:03:54.248 --> 00:03:56.973
These failures rarely throw clear errors.

62
00:03:56.973 --> 00:04:00.861
A certificate doesn't renew because
enrollment was never re-pointed.

63
00:04:00.861 --> 00:04:05.010
A device fails network access because
a group lookup comes back empty.

64
00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:09.760
An LDAP application stops because its
bind account was deleted with the domain.

65
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.548
Organizations rarely fail
because they can't list objects.

66
00:04:13.548 --> 00:04:18.087
They fail because nobody treats five
steps as a discipline: enumerate,

67
00:04:18.087 --> 00:04:22.000
map each dependency to a target,
keep delegation continuous,

68
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.880
deliver through a governed
pipeline, and detect drift.

69
00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:27.918
Now, what changes.

70
00:04:27.918 --> 00:04:31.780
Before anything moves, the
program needs one place where

71
00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:35.479
every decision is written
down, signed and replayable.

72
00:04:35.479 --> 00:04:39.406
Chapter 03 describes a
reference platform host:

73
00:04:39.406 --> 00:04:45.392
one Windows Server 2025 server, supported
to November 2034, with a standby.

74
00:04:45.392 --> 00:04:50.417
The host has five roles: a version-controlled
source of truth with signed commits;

75
00:04:50.417 --> 00:04:55.442
a reverse proxy for TLS and client
certificates; an authentication bridge to the

76
00:04:55.442 --> 00:05:00.542
legacy forest; the transformation engine;
and an adapter per dependency category.

77
00:05:00.542 --> 00:05:03.430
What matters is a single
authoritative writer.

78
00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:06.068
Governance and execution never mix.

79
00:05:06.068 --> 00:05:10.129
One layer validates and signs
plans but never touches a target.

80
00:05:10.129 --> 00:05:13.708
The other runs authorized
plans but decides nothing,

81
00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:16.880
under separate accounts,
credentials and logs.

82
00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.718
The host reads Active Directory
read-only and is protected as Tier 0.

83
00:05:21.718 --> 00:05:25.405
Book 05 covers servers
under Azure Arc in depth,

84
00:05:25.405 --> 00:05:29.450
and Book 04 covers change
as code with evidence.

85
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.075
Identity comes first, because every
later control reads the attributes.

86
00:05:35.075 --> 00:05:39.934
For the workforce, the authoritative
source is the HR system of record,

87
00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:46.050
and OMB M-22-09 requires centralized
identity management for agency users.

88
00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:50.987
Four mechanisms rebuild what the OU tree
did: attributes replace OU placement,

89
00:05:50.987 --> 00:05:55.772
dynamic groups replace OU-derived
groups, administrative units replace OU

90
00:05:55.772 --> 00:06:01.013
delegation, and Conditional Access
targeting replaces Group Policy inheritance.

91
00:06:01.013 --> 00:06:06.198
The program's contribution is the contract
that binds them: one HR-sourced stamp

92
00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:11.775
drives membership, delegated scope, policy
targeting, licensing and drift detection.

93
00:06:11.775 --> 00:06:12.938
Two cautions.

94
00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:16.600
Dynamic membership usually
updates within a few hours but

95
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.113
can take more than 24, so design
movers around that window.

96
00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:24.262
And HR provisioning must be the
only writer of those attributes.

97
00:06:24.262 --> 00:06:26.474
Tokens only work if this is right.

98
00:06:26.474 --> 00:06:31.120
Books 02 and 03 carry identity in depth.

99
00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.118
Group Policy was Active Directory's
most ambitious delivery system,

100
00:06:36.118 --> 00:06:37.758
and its most network-bound.

101
00:06:37.758 --> 00:06:41.632
A client applies it only when it
can reach a domain controller,

102
00:06:41.632 --> 00:06:43.683
and every refresh is a trip to one.

103
00:06:43.683 --> 00:06:49.008
A cloud-native endpoint, Entra joined
and in Intune, has no such dependency.

104
00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:52.285
Don't port a fifty-setting
GPO as fifty settings;

105
00:06:52.285 --> 00:06:56.570
that carries dead weight, old
conflicts and manual targeting.

106
00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:00.620
Microsoft's recommended option is
to start new rather than migrate.

107
00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:06.241
So the program regenerates one canonical
policy per category, keyed to attributes,

108
00:07:06.241 --> 00:07:09.500
into Intune, Conditional
Access and, for servers,

109
00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:14.632
Azure machine configuration, because
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

110
00:07:14.632 --> 00:07:17.925
Every change passes four
stages: validation,

111
00:07:17.925 --> 00:07:22.020
a simulated tenant, a report-only
pilot, then production.

112
00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.714
And prefer require to block,
except where the federal SCuBA

113
00:07:25.714 --> 00:07:29.182
baseline demands a block, as
for legacy authentication.

114
00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:32.339
Book 05 covers the device detail in depth,

115
00:07:32.339 --> 00:07:36.030
and Book 04 the
Conditional Access library.

116
00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.800
DNS, DHCP and address management are what

117
00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.530
agencies most often defer,
because they still work.

118
00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.017
That's how they keep
the old directory alive.

119
00:07:46.017 --> 00:07:49.648
AD-integrated zones replicate
with Active Directory,

120
00:07:49.648 --> 00:07:54.893
and domain-joined clients find domain
controllers through records Netlogon

121
00:07:54.893 --> 00:07:59.492
registers in DNS, so DNS and AD
retirement must be sequenced together.

122
00:07:59.492 --> 00:08:04.443
The target is a governed authority outside
AD, Azure DNS Private Resolver as a

123
00:08:04.443 --> 00:08:07.305
forwarding bridge rather
than an authority,

124
00:08:07.305 --> 00:08:10.167
and Azure private zones
for cloud workloads.

125
00:08:10.167 --> 00:08:15.243
A Windows DHCP server checks its
authorization in AD every hour.

126
00:08:15.243 --> 00:08:19.368
That stops an unauthorized Windows
server, not a rogue device.

127
00:08:19.368 --> 00:08:25.343
And one IPAM authority owns the address
space; DNS and DHCP derive from it.

128
00:08:25.343 --> 00:08:28.829
Leave the three for "Phase
2" and it rarely comes.

129
00:08:28.829 --> 00:08:32.805
A forest that still hosts
DNS hasn't been retired.

130
00:08:32.805 --> 00:08:38.660
Book 08 covers the design in depth,
and Book 09 the network underneath.

131
00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:42.365
Once identity, policy
and services have moved,

132
00:08:42.365 --> 00:08:46.798
domain-joined PCs and servers are
the largest remaining dependency.

133
00:08:46.798 --> 00:08:50.186
User PCs end Entra joined and in Intune.

134
00:08:50.186 --> 00:08:53.610
Servers come under Azure Arc
and machine configuration.

135
00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.585
Hybrid join is a waypoint.

136
00:08:55.585 --> 00:08:58.601
Microsoft says it shouldn't
be anyone's end state,

137
00:08:58.601 --> 00:09:02.974
and there's no supported way to
convert a hybrid-joined device to Entra

138
00:09:02.974 --> 00:09:07.347
join without resetting Windows, so
time the move to a hardware refresh.

139
00:09:07.347 --> 00:09:12.217
Every service principal name is an
application that expects Kerberos,

140
00:09:12.217 --> 00:09:16.509
and each needs a decision: migrate
it to modern authentication,

141
00:09:16.509 --> 00:09:20.635
retire it, or, as a last resort,
island it with an end-of-life date.

142
00:09:20.635 --> 00:09:25.221
No server leaves the domain until
every SPN on it is resolved,

143
00:09:25.221 --> 00:09:27.422
and NTLM goes in the same pass.

144
00:09:27.422 --> 00:09:32.334
And compute stays with its data until
profiling shows the round trips are reduced.

145
00:09:32.334 --> 00:09:36.730
Books 05 and 06 carry the detail in depth.

146
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.555
When Active Directory retires, being
inside the network stops meaning anything.

147
00:09:42.555 --> 00:09:48.367
OMB M-22-09 says users should log into
applications rather than networks.

148
00:09:48.367 --> 00:09:54.081
The replacement has four surfaces: Zero
Trust as the model, Conditional Access as

149
00:09:54.081 --> 00:09:59.880
the per-request decision, SASE as the edge,
and phishing-resistant MFA as the proof.

150
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:05.261
M-22-09 requires that MFA for
staff, contractors and partners,

151
00:10:05.261 --> 00:10:10.818
and BOD 25-01 makes blocking
legacy authentication mandatory.

152
00:10:10.818 --> 00:10:12.006
Smart cards stay.

153
00:10:12.006 --> 00:10:17.497
Entra certificate-based authentication
moves validation from the domain controller

154
00:10:17.497 --> 00:10:20.657
to Entra ID, but a card
counts as phishing-resistant

155
00:10:20.657 --> 00:10:23.667
only if the binding policy
makes it multifactor.

156
00:10:23.667 --> 00:10:29.279
And issuance needs a successor first,
because an enterprise CA depends on AD DS.

157
00:10:29.279 --> 00:10:33.889
Administrators hold no standing privilege:
roles are eligible in Privileged

158
00:10:33.889 --> 00:10:37.930
Identity Management, and a vault covers
what can't be made just in time.

159
00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:42.137
Book 04 covers the policy
and privilege model in depth,

160
00:10:42.137 --> 00:10:44.740
and Book 07 the certificates.

161
00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:46.353
Who leads it?

162
00:10:46.353 --> 00:10:50.905
Chapter 09 puts it in one line:
Microsoft provides the instruments;

163
00:10:50.905 --> 00:10:53.389
the program provides the orchestra.

164
00:10:53.389 --> 00:10:55.051
The tools are each excellent,

165
00:10:55.051 --> 00:10:59.343
but none of them decides what the
organizational structure should become,

166
00:10:59.343 --> 00:11:03.565
or what to do with the findings, and no
vendor deadline forces the decision.

167
00:11:03.565 --> 00:11:05.365
So name one decider per row.

168
00:11:05.365 --> 00:11:11.453
The sponsor and CIO own scope, funding,
sequence and when AD components retire.

169
00:11:11.453 --> 00:11:15.372
The authorizing official is the
only official who can accept risk,

170
00:11:15.372 --> 00:11:18.864
so every deferred cutoff and
Kerberos island reaches them.

171
00:11:18.864 --> 00:11:21.265
The CISO sets control requirements.

172
00:11:21.265 --> 00:11:26.077
The program office and its steward hold
the repository and clear most gates.

173
00:11:26.077 --> 00:11:31.285
HR decides what worker attributes mean,
application owners sign off their cutovers,

174
00:11:31.285 --> 00:11:36.640
and the records officer is consulted before
any domain controller is decommissioned.

175
00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:42.600
Each month, ask about the drift trend,
signed changes and open risk acceptances.

176
00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:44.375
How do you get there?

177
00:11:44.375 --> 00:11:45.913
Start with an inventory.

178
00:11:45.913 --> 00:11:50.081
You can't modernize what you haven't
enumerated, and nothing is retired

179
00:11:50.081 --> 00:11:53.675
until it's written down, read-only,
with proof of how it was read.

180
00:11:53.675 --> 00:11:56.672
The assessment account
holds no write rights,

181
00:11:56.672 --> 00:12:00.537
the code imports no write
commands, and every call is logged.

182
00:12:00.537 --> 00:12:05.869
A default authenticated account already
reads more than most teams expect: the

183
00:12:05.869 --> 00:12:11.200
topology, the OU tree, most Group Policy,
users, groups and certificate templates.

184
00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.925
A narrow second tier adds only
server-side exports, never secrets.

185
00:12:15.925 --> 00:12:21.350
Every run records who, when, against
what, and a hash of every file.

186
00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.738
Eleven streams feed the risk scoring.

187
00:12:23.738 --> 00:12:30.671
One example: Windows Server 2012 extended
updates through Arc ended on October 13,

188
00:12:30.671 --> 00:12:36.176
2026, and Windows Server 2016
reaches end of support on January 12,

189
00:12:36.176 --> 00:12:40.050
2027, so every such server
needs a disposition.

190
00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:44.660
And no application leaves its data
until its transactions are profiled.

191
00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:49.660
Chapter 11 turns the inventory
into change in three stages.

192
00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.822
Analyze builds current-state and
target-state graphs and a diff.

193
00:12:53.822 --> 00:12:58.235
Plan expands the diff into typed
actions, each scoped to one object,

194
00:12:58.235 --> 00:13:01.685
ordered by dependency and
paired with its rollback.

195
00:13:01.685 --> 00:13:03.523
A plan must pass validation,

196
00:13:03.523 --> 00:13:07.760
then authorization by an approver
of record who isn't the author.

197
00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.249
Only then does a separate
execution identity deliver it,

198
00:13:11.249 --> 00:13:14.960
verifying every action and
closing with an evidence packet.

199
00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:20.311
The actuation ladder caps how far
automation may go: record, observe,

200
00:13:20.311 --> 00:13:26.235
advise, gated actuation, and autonomy
only for enumerated low-risk classes.

201
00:13:26.235 --> 00:13:28.757
Gated actuation is the default ceiling,

202
00:13:28.757 --> 00:13:32.272
because it's a posture an
authorizing official can sign.

203
00:13:32.272 --> 00:13:35.431
CISA calls its maturity
model a general guide,

204
00:13:35.431 --> 00:13:39.560
not a strict set of requirements,
so this is design, not mandate.

205
00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:44.120
When nothing has drifted, the next
run produces a zero-action plan.

206
00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:50.170
Chapter 12 sequences everything as an
illustrative fifty-two-week program: seven

207
00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:55.470
phases, from pre-flight to steady state,
with five workstreams running in parallel.

208
00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.550
Large federal estates
typically run multi-year;

209
00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:01.707
the weeks are a sequencing
model, not a commitment.

210
00:14:01.707 --> 00:14:06.614
A phase opens only when the last gate
clears, and gates read from data:

211
00:14:06.614 --> 00:14:10.557
"zero drift for fourteen days"
is a query, not a signature.

212
00:14:10.557 --> 00:14:12.685
The steward clears most gates;

213
00:14:12.685 --> 00:14:17.620
the sponsor and CIO sign the one that
says Active Directory is retired.

214
00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:22.195
Under it all is one structural move:
separate truth from enforcement.

215
00:14:22.195 --> 00:14:26.052
A governance layer holds and
reconciles authoritative state,

216
00:14:26.052 --> 00:14:30.057
while identity, network and device
platforms enforce at runtime.

217
00:14:30.057 --> 00:14:35.247
Five principles follow: one authoritative
source, application-aware networking,

218
00:14:35.247 --> 00:14:40.132
token-based identity, governing actively,
and fewer synchronous round trips.

219
00:14:40.132 --> 00:14:42.216
And read one clock correctly:

220
00:14:42.216 --> 00:14:46.557
FedRAMP's 2027 date governs
cloud providers' certification.

221
00:14:46.557 --> 00:14:49.870
The agency still authorizes
its own systems.

222
00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:51.995
So, four decisions.

223
00:14:51.995 --> 00:14:55.832
Name the owners: one decider per
row, starting with the sponsor,

224
00:14:55.832 --> 00:14:58.465
the authorizing official and the steward,

225
00:14:58.465 --> 00:15:04.183
and confirm who holds the zero trust
implementation lead role M-22-09 required.

226
00:15:04.183 --> 00:15:06.137
Run the read-only assessment,

227
00:15:06.137 --> 00:15:10.045
across all eleven categories,
committed with provenance.

228
00:15:10.045 --> 00:15:14.582
Stand up the platform host, so every
change has one writer and a plan.

229
00:15:14.582 --> 00:15:17.847
And set the dates: gate dates
on the sponsor's calendar,

230
00:15:17.847 --> 00:15:21.557
and every exception's end date
on the authorizing official's.

231
00:15:21.557 --> 00:15:24.645
None of this requires a
vendor decision on day one.

232
00:15:24.645 --> 00:15:27.732
Start with an inventory,
an owner, and a date.

233
00:15:27.732 --> 00:15:31.708
Modernization is a governance
rebuild, not a platform swap.

234
00:15:31.708 --> 00:15:37.018
Book 01 is the map, and Books 02
to 09 carry each part in depth,

235
00:15:37.018 --> 00:15:39.120
every claim footnoted.

236
00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:39.940
Thank you.
